// Structs that reach themselves, directly or through each other.
//
// All three forms were refused until analysis learned to answer a type that is
// still being laid out instead of refusing it. Every re-entry was an error
// regardless of what had been asked, so Cone could not express a linked list or
// a tree at all -- 'struct S { next &S }' was rejected along with the genuinely
// infinite 'struct S { inner S }'.
//
// What tells them apart is not recursion but size, and it belongs to the type
// being asked rather than to the field asking. A reference answers its own size
// -- a pointer is a pointer whatever it points at -- and never consults its
// target, so a cycle passing through one always terminates. A field holding a
// struct by value does consult it, and a struct mid-layout has nothing to say.
// struct-typecheck-decl holds the by-value form that is still refused.
//
// 'compile' rather than 'run': traversing a list needs a nullable reference,
// which is a union with an empty variant, and the union machinery would be most
// of the file. What matters here is that these declarations are accepted and
// reach code generation as genuinely self-referential types, which the named
// check in cases.toml asserts against the LLVM IR -- runtime could not see it.

// -------- direct: a linked list --------

// The list node the whole rule exists for. 'next' is a reference, so laying out
// Node never asks Node for its size.
struct Node {
  v i64
  next &Node
}

fn nodeNext(n &Node) i64 {
  n.next.v
}

// -------- mixed: one by value, one by reference --------

// Owner holds Held by value, so Held must be sized -- and it is, because its
// own field is a reference. The cycle is broken on the second hop rather than
// the first, which is the case that says the rule is about each field's own
// type and not about whether a cycle exists anywhere.
struct Owner {
  v i64
  held Held
}

struct Held {
  back &Owner
}

fn ownerBack(o &Owner) i64 {
  o.held.back.v
}

// -------- pure: neither asks the other --------

struct Left {
  v i64
  r &Right
}

struct Right {
  l &Left
}

fn leftRound(l &Left) i64 {
  l.r.l.v
}
